# Line endings.
#
# This repository is edited on Windows and RUN on a Linux cluster, so any file the
# cluster executes has to keep LF regardless of the local checkout. Without this,
# git's autocrlf hands the working tree CRLF, a tar of that tree carries CRLF to the
# cluster, and bash fails in ways that do not look like a line-ending problem:
#
#   hpc/run_pipeline.sh: line 16: set: pipefail: invalid option name
#   hpc/run_pipeline.sh: line 17: cd: $'hpc/..\r': No such file or directory
#   hpc/run_pipeline.sh: line 43: syntax error near unexpected token `newline'
#
# Python and YAML tolerate CRLF through universal newlines; shell does not, and a
# `#!/bin/bash\r` shebang makes the kernel look for an interpreter named "bash\r".
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.bash    text eol=lf
run_tnk1.sh text eol=lf

# Slurm reads these the same way bash does.
*.sbatch  text eol=lf

# Keep the rest normalised in the repository and native in the checkout.
*.py      text
*.yaml    text
*.yml     text
*.toml    text
*.cfg     text
*.ini     text
*.md      text
*.txt     text
*.json    text
*.csv     text

# Binary: never touch, never diff.
*.parquet binary
*.npz     binary
*.npy     binary
*.pt      binary
*.pkl     binary
*.db      binary
*.pdf     binary
*.png     binary
*.zip     binary
*.gz      binary
*.tar.gz  binary
*.onnx    binary
*.hdf5    binary
*.ipynb   binary
*.pdb     binary
*.pdbqt   binary
*.cif     binary
